<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <assemblyIdentity
      type="win32"
      name="PandaKoder.LangSwitcher"
      version="0.96.0.0"
      processorArchitecture="amd64"/>

  <!-- asInvoker, with UI access explicitly not requested. This is the manifest of TWO
       images, and section 8.2 of SPEC is the reason for both: Windows grants an image that
       requests UI access its privilege only through ShellExecute, and only when the machine
       trusts its signature AND it lies in %ProgramFiles%. CreateProcess refuses such an image
       with error 740 in any folder, whatever its signature; ShellExecute refuses it with error
       8235 when the signature is not trusted (build.rs lists the measured refusals).

       1. The Debug build, cargo test and cargo run. The test harness is built from the same
          bin target, and the Release manifest would make cargo test fail before a single
          test runs. Measured in task H-9, not assumed.

       2. The BASE image of the delivery, LangSwitcher-base.exe (stage E91, question 155):
          the same Release built with LANGSW_NO_UIACCESS=1. It starts on any machine; the
          hotkey does not reach the windows of programs run as administrator. The installer
          puts it down first and keeps it wherever the full image cannot be trusted.
          tools\verify-one-body.ps1 proves that the two Release images differ in this
          resource and in the fields derived from the hash of the content, and nowhere else.

       Two notes for whoever edits this file.

       First: an XML comment may not contain a double hyphen. Two hyphens in a row here
       produce a manifest that parses nowhere, and Windows reports it as the wholly
       unhelpful error 14001, side-by-side configuration is incorrect. TOOLCHAIN.md
       section 8.4.

       Second: do not spell the release attribute out literally anywhere in this file,
       not even inside a comment. The controller tool verify-resources.ps1 searches the
       raw manifest text for that attribute and would report the Debug build as carrying
       UI access. -->
  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges>
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="asInvoker" uiAccess="false"/>
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>

  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <!-- Windows 10 and 11. Section 3 of SPEC: minimum is Windows 10 1607, because
           ToUnicodeEx needs the 0x4 flag. -->
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}"/>
    </application>
  </compatibility>

  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2</dpiAwareness>
      <activeCodePage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2019/WindowsSettings">UTF-8</activeCodePage>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
